WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.867
Open the NanoCoFold release on GitHub.

2
00:00:02.987 --> 00:00:08.661
On an Ubuntu computer with an NVIDIA GPU, you can
install it from the source ZIP or with Git.

3
00:00:08.781 --> 00:00:09.891
Here we use Git.

4
00:00:10.658 --> 00:00:13.346
Open a terminal and run nvidia-smi.

5
00:00:13.466 --> 00:00:16.112
Check that it lists your GPU and its driver.

6
00:00:16.232 --> 00:00:21.266
Before installing, check the memory and free disk
space required in the release README.

7
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.145
Copy the Git clone command from the guide.

8
00:00:24.265 --> 00:00:29.044
It downloads the release into a folder called
NanoCoFold_public.

9
00:00:29.164 --> 00:00:31.105
Wait for the command prompt to return.

10
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:33.296
Change into that folder.

11
00:00:33.416 --> 00:00:36.701
Check that Git prints v0.4.8.

12
00:00:36.821 --> 00:00:39.403
You should see the installer and the start script
here.

13
00:00:39.523 --> 00:00:44.472
If you downloaded the ZIP, extract it and open a
terminal inside the extracted folder.

14
00:00:45.242 --> 00:00:48.527
Choose one installation command for the work you
want to do.

15
00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:53.426
The guide lists the first complex example and the
full installation side by side.

16
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.253
For the first complex example, type the complex
command and press Enter.

17
00:00:58.373 --> 00:01:01.424
It installs the application, Boltz-2 and Chai-1.

18
00:01:01.544 --> 00:01:04.595
If Ubuntu asks for a password, enter it in the
terminal.

19
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:08.478
For all four modes, including new nanobody design

20
00:01:08.503 --> 00:01:11.895
and variant evaluation, use the all command
instead.

21
00:01:12.015 --> 00:01:14.468
You only need to choose one of these two profiles.

22
00:01:15.242 --> 00:01:18.548
Here is the installer running on a fresh copy of
the release.

23
00:01:18.668 --> 00:01:21.868
For this demonstration we install the application
alone.

24
00:01:21.988 --> 00:01:26.234
Our Ubuntu computer already has the system
packages, so it skips that step.

25
00:01:26.992 --> 00:01:32.176
Wait for the final line that says installation
complete, followed by bash start.sh.

26
00:01:32.296 --> 00:01:35.090
The core installation shown here prepares the web
app.

27
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:38.901
A GPU profile also installs its prediction
engines.

28
00:01:39.658 --> 00:01:44.992
With a GPU profile, leave the terminal open while
it downloads the engines and models.

29
00:01:45.112 --> 00:01:48.610
Here is the completion message from a saved full
installation.

30
00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.760
Wait for it on your own computer before starting
the server.

31
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:54.880
In the same folder, run the start command.

32
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.715
Keep this terminal open and copy the web address
printed after NanoCoFold.

33
00:01:59.835 --> 00:02:04.528
This first launch was recorded on a fresh
installation of the application itself.

34
00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:08.078
Open the address in a browser on the same
computer.

35
00:02:08.198 --> 00:02:12.187
If the port is busy, use port zero and open the
new address it prints.

36
00:02:12.307 --> 00:02:16.489
For a remote computer, follow the SSH instructions
in the README.

37
00:02:17.242 --> 00:02:22.554
For your first analysis, open the environment
check and choose Predict protein complexes.

38
00:02:22.674 --> 00:02:26.002
After your selected engines pass, click Prepare
analysis.

39
00:02:26.122 --> 00:02:29.706
You can now load the public example and follow the
full walkthrough.
